functions.detail:
Which of the two comes back is detected from the file's bytes, never from its extension, so a mislabelled picture is still a picture. Narrow the result before use:

```csharp
if (Files.ReadFile("logo.png") is Files.ImageFile picture)
{
    Views.OpenText("logo", $"{picture.Label}, {picture.Bytes} bytes");
}
```

A relative path resolves against the workspace; an absolute one is read as given, so anything else in this container is readable. Reading an image describes it — label, media type, byte size — and shows nothing: the pixels reach neither the program nor the context window.

Returns: the file's text window, or the picture's description.

Throws `ToolException`: `ToolErrorCode.NotFound` for a missing path.

A non-zero exit is an ordinary result rather than a failure of the call: the output and the code both come back, so a program branches on them. Only a failure to launch the process, or the timeout killing it, throws.

```csharp
var built = Shell.Run("cargo build --release");
if (built.ExitCode != 0)
{
    Views.OpenText("build", built.Output);
}
```

Throws `ToolException`: `ToolErrorCode.LimitExceeded` when the timeout killed it, and `ToolErrorCode.IOError` when the process could not be started at all.

This is how the result of a program reaches the model that wrote it. Opening the same label again replaces what it showed, so a label names a thing rather than a moment.

```csharp
var tests = Shell.Run("cargo nextest run --workspace");
Views.OpenText("tests", tests.Output);
```

Throws `ToolException`: `ToolErrorCode.InvalidArgument` for an empty label, and `ToolErrorCode.LimitExceeded` naming the cap a body or label went over.

types.detail:
It is exactly one of the two, and that is the whole reason it is a type rather than two nullable arguments: gg can carry out neither "both" nor "neither", and this type makes both of those a compile error rather than a refusal at run time.

```csharp
Delegation.SpawnSubagent("reviewer", Delegation.Brief.Issue("AUTH-3"));
Delegation.SpawnSubagent("worker", Delegation.Brief.Prompt("port the fixtures"));
```

It exists so that clearing a description and setting it to the empty string stop being one request. The default is `Tasks.TextEdit.Keep`, so an update that does not mention a description does not touch it. `Board.UpdateIssue` takes the same value for the same reason.

```csharp
Tasks.UpdateTask("build", description: Tasks.TextEdit.Set("cargo build --release"));
Tasks.UpdateTask("build", description: Tasks.TextEdit.Clear);
```

A failure is thrown rather than returned because C# is an exception language: the common path reads as a straight line, and only the calls expected to fail are wrapped.

```csharp
try
{
    Views.OpenText("notes", Files.ReadTextFile("NOTES.md"));
}
catch (ToolException failure) when (failure.Code == ToolErrorCode.NotFound)
{
    Views.OpenText("notes", "there are no notes yet");
}
```

An unexpected one is best left to escape: gg reports which call failed, with the managed stack, and the turn is recoverable.
